Drop the truthiness test on a va_list, which does not compile on aarch64.

va_list is an array type on aarch64, so `__args` in a boolean context is
an array-to-pointer decay GCC rejects outright:

    src/log.c:124:22: error: invalid operands to binary && (have 'int' and 'va_list')

On x86_64 va_list is a pointer, the test is vacuously true, and it builds -
which is why upstream has not noticed. The test was never meaningful
either way: every caller of log_line() runs va_start immediately before
it, so __args is always valid, and __format alone is the real guard.

Reported upstream is worth doing; until then the aarch64 leg of our build
fails here and way-displays is absent from the aarch64 repository.

--- a/src/log.c
+++ b/src/log.c
@@ -121,7 +121,7 @@
 	char *l;
 
 	// allocate the line for output and capture
-	if (__format && __args)
+	if (__format)
 		l = vsnprintf_alloc(MAX_LINE_LEN, __format, __args);
 	else
 		l = strdup("");
